WEBVTT

00:00.656 --> 00:02.502
- [Instructor] What we're
going to do in this video

00:02.502 --> 00:03.920
is introduce ourselves

00:03.920 --> 00:05.670
to the quotient rule.

00:06.535 --> 00:08.854
And we're not going to
prove it in this video.

00:08.854 --> 00:10.837
In a future video we can prove
it using the product rule

00:10.837 --> 00:13.581
and we'll see it has some
similarities to the product rule.

00:13.581 --> 00:15.681
But here, we'll learn about what it is

00:15.681 --> 00:19.032
and how and where to actually apply it.

00:19.032 --> 00:20.639
So for example

00:20.639 --> 00:22.803
if I have some function F of X

00:22.803 --> 00:25.019
and it can be expressed as the quotient

00:25.019 --> 00:26.125
of two expressions.

00:26.125 --> 00:27.324
So let's say

00:27.324 --> 00:28.157
U of X

00:29.074 --> 00:30.074
over V of X.

00:32.196 --> 00:34.233
Then the quotient rule tells us

00:34.233 --> 00:36.374
that F prime of X

00:36.374 --> 00:38.101
is going to be equal to

00:38.101 --> 00:39.753
and this is going to look
a little bit complicated

00:39.753 --> 00:41.496
but once we apply it, you'll hopefully

00:41.496 --> 00:43.320
get a little bit more comfortable with it.

00:43.320 --> 00:45.041
Its going to be equal to the derivative

00:45.041 --> 00:46.635
of the numerator function.

00:46.635 --> 00:48.404
U prime of X.

00:48.404 --> 00:51.051
Times the denominator function.

00:51.051 --> 00:52.292
V of X.

00:52.292 --> 00:53.125
Minus

00:54.437 --> 00:56.482
the numerator function.

00:56.482 --> 00:57.315
U of X.

00:58.874 --> 01:00.654
Do that in that blue color.

01:00.654 --> 01:01.487
U of X.

01:02.535 --> 01:04.870
Times the derivative of
the denominator function

01:04.870 --> 01:07.168
times V prime of X.

01:07.168 --> 01:09.913
And this already looks very
similar to the product rule.

01:09.913 --> 01:11.998
If this was U of X times V of X

01:11.998 --> 01:14.040
then this is what we would
get if we took the derivative

01:14.040 --> 01:15.258
this was a plus sign.

01:15.258 --> 01:16.809
But this is here, a minus sign.

01:16.809 --> 01:17.900
But were not done yet.

01:17.900 --> 01:19.817
We would then divide by

01:21.189 --> 01:23.723
the denominator function squared.

01:23.723 --> 01:24.556
V of X

01:26.646 --> 01:27.880
squared.

01:27.880 --> 01:31.510
So let's actually apply this idea.

01:31.510 --> 01:34.659
So let's say that we have F of X

01:34.659 --> 01:36.393
is equal to

01:36.393 --> 01:37.646
X squared

01:37.646 --> 01:39.063
over cosine of X.

01:40.739 --> 01:42.371
Well what could be our U of X

01:42.371 --> 01:43.774
and what could be our V of X?

01:43.774 --> 01:44.902
Well, our U of X

01:44.902 --> 01:46.417
could be our X squared.

01:46.417 --> 01:47.834
So that is U of X

01:48.990 --> 01:52.407
and U prime of X would be equal to two X.

01:53.415 --> 01:56.243
And then this could be our V of X.

01:56.243 --> 01:57.743
So this is V of X.

01:58.954 --> 02:00.961
And V prime of X.

02:00.961 --> 02:03.117
The derivative of cosine
of X with respect to X

02:03.117 --> 02:06.171
is equal to negative sine of X.

02:06.171 --> 02:07.844
And then we just apply this.

02:07.844 --> 02:09.154
So based on that

02:09.154 --> 02:10.154
F prime of X

02:11.336 --> 02:12.726
is going to be equal to

02:12.726 --> 02:15.156
the derivative of the numerator function

02:15.156 --> 02:18.353
that's two X, right over
here, that's that there.

02:18.353 --> 02:19.639
So it's gonna be two X

02:19.639 --> 02:21.591
times the denominator function.

02:21.591 --> 02:23.972
V of X is just cosine of X

02:23.972 --> 02:25.472
times cosine of X.

02:26.473 --> 02:27.306
Minus

02:28.281 --> 02:29.676
the numerator function

02:29.676 --> 02:32.014
which is just X squared.

02:32.014 --> 02:33.605
X squared.

02:33.605 --> 02:36.518
Times the derivative of
the denominator function.

02:36.518 --> 02:37.935
The derivative of cosine of X

02:37.935 --> 02:39.561
is negative sine X.

02:39.561 --> 02:40.918
So, negative

02:40.918 --> 02:41.751
sine of X.

02:43.377 --> 02:45.240
All of that over

02:45.240 --> 02:46.573
all of that over

02:47.508 --> 02:49.902
the denominator function squared.

02:49.902 --> 02:52.256
So that's cosine of X

02:52.256 --> 02:54.070
and I'm going to square it.

02:54.070 --> 02:55.963
I could write it, of course, like this.

02:55.963 --> 02:57.320
Actually, let me write it like that

02:57.320 --> 02:58.678
just to make it a little bit clearer.

02:58.678 --> 03:01.362
And at this point, we
just have to simplify.

03:01.362 --> 03:04.134
This is going to be equal to

03:04.134 --> 03:05.785
let's see, we're gonna get

03:05.785 --> 03:07.785
two X times cosine of X.

03:08.890 --> 03:10.377
Two X

03:10.377 --> 03:11.377
cosine of X.

03:12.334 --> 03:15.328
Negative times a negative is a positive.

03:15.328 --> 03:16.578
Plus, X squared

03:18.084 --> 03:19.639
X squared

03:19.639 --> 03:20.972
times sine of X.

03:21.913 --> 03:23.297
Sine

03:23.297 --> 03:24.304
of X.

03:24.304 --> 03:25.637
All of that over

03:27.071 --> 03:29.310
cosine of X squared.

03:29.310 --> 03:31.280
Which I could write like this, as well.

03:31.280 --> 03:32.414
And we're done.

03:32.414 --> 03:33.826
You could try to simplify it, in fact,

03:33.826 --> 03:36.654
there's not an obvious way
to simplify this any further.

03:36.654 --> 03:38.257
Now what you'll see in the future

03:38.257 --> 03:39.662
you might already know something called

03:39.662 --> 03:42.000
the chain rule, or you might
learn it in the future.

03:42.000 --> 03:43.749
But you could also do the quotient rule

03:43.749 --> 03:45.529
using the product and the chain rule

03:45.529 --> 03:47.167
that you might learn in the future.

03:47.167 --> 03:48.558
But if you don't know the chain rule yet,

03:48.558 --> 03:50.475
this is fairly useful.
